We revise and implement a Galilean-covariant dispersion equation for high-viscosity relativistic fluids, ensuring its consistency with linearized hydrodynamic modes in various inertial reference frames. Originally formulated by Bardeen and Mottola using frame-independent invariants to linear order, we reformulate the equation using frame-dependent but physically grounded quantities. Specifically, we express the fluid's average flow velocity via linearized Landau frames and define the dissipation tensor relative to a Galilean frame of rest for the perturbation. We validate our approach by applying the equation to modes predicted by the linearized relativistic Navier-Stokes and Müller-Israel-Starr hydrodynamics. We further analyze the equation in the context of holographic duals within the AdS/CFT correspondence, specifically addressing recent findings regarding Eddington-Finkelstein coordinate singularities in bulk-to-boundary propagators. Our results confirm that the causality cones associated with perturbation growths governed by our equation align precisely with those derived by the associated holographic dynamics in global AdS spacetime.
